"content_id": "23159",
"reference_id": 22327,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "Philemon 1:4–7",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "57001004-57001007",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "Professional",
"content": "<p>In his letters, Paul usually thanks God for the faith and love of the people he writes to. He also tells them that he prays for them (<a href=\"https://ref.ly/Rom1:8-Rom1:12\">Romans 1:8–12</a>; <a href=\"https://ref.ly/1Cor1:4-1Cor1:9\">1&nbsp;Corinthians 1:4–9</a>; <a href=\"https://ref.ly/2Cor1:3-2Cor1:11\">2&nbsp;Corinthians 1:3–11</a>; contrast <a href=\"https://ref.ly/Gal1:6-Gal1:10\">Galatians 1:6–10</a>). Ancient Greek letters sometimes included a thanksgiving section after the greeting.</p>",
"associations": {
"passage": [
{
"start_ref": "57001004",
"start_ref_usfm": "PHM 1:4",
"end_ref": "57001007",
"end_ref_usfm": "PHM 1:7"
